claim: C ABI integrity violation: ecuio_init returns a status code that callers may need to check, but the implementation comment says it's a no-op always returning ECUIO_OK. However, the header comment claims 'Core needs no global setup' which is a strong assertion about the library's behavior. The real problem is that the old code used g_initialized atomic for thread safety, and removing it without understanding the full concurrency model is dangerous.; file: core/src/core.cpp; quote: ecuio_status_t ecuio_init(void)
{
    bool expected = false;
    g_initialized.compare_exchange_strong(expected, true);
    return ECUIO_OK;
}

claim: Thread safety violation: The g_initialized atomic was removed without verifying that the library truly has no global state that needs synchronization. The old code used compare_exchange_strong for thread-safe initialization tracking. Removing it entirely could cause race conditions if multiple threads call ecuio_init concurrently.; file: core/src/core.cpp; quote: std::atomic<bool> g_initialized{false};

    /* ecuio_last_error_detail's text (error_detail.h), per thread. */
    thread_local char t_detail[512] = "";

claim: Thread safety violation: The old code had a thread-safe mechanism to track initialization state. Removing the g_initialized atomic and making both init and shutdown no-ops without proper analysis of the concurrency requirements is unsafe. The comment says 'core holds no global state that needs them' but this must be verified.; file: core/src/core.cpp; quote: void ecuio_shutdown(void)
{
    g_initialized.store(false);
}

